{% decision_card number="ADR-0019" status="approved" %}
Phase 1.6 PR-0 increment authorized 2026-08-11; the full PR-0–PR-8 sequence is not pre-authorized.
{% /decision_card %}

## Context

Phase 1.5 is verified **complete-frozen**. A live re-run of `npm run phase15:audit` against `main` at commit `10f6907` returned `localReady`, `publicationReady`, and `externalReady` all `true` with every `failedGates` array empty and the closure sentence "Phase 1.5 closed. Beacon may proceed to the next business-domain/UI phase." This is the live-verified precondition the Phase 1.6 master spec's own Section 0.1 start rule requires before any Phase 1.6 implementation.

The repository owner supplied `src/content/docs/plans/phase-1-6-master-spec.mdoc` (V5.0), a 35-section-plus-appendices architecture document covering the knowledge/research/understanding/decision lifecycle, event-sourced lineage, the four-layer memory model, provenance, provider continuity (the Capacity Watcher), and a bounded PR-0 through PR-8 implementation sequence (Section 31). It was conformed to this repository's real Markdoc conventions (frontmatter, diagram catalog, mermaid rendering) and landed as `truthState: target` planning content, not as an ADR — per the document's own Section 19 authority invariant, a landed planning document is research and concept, not authorization.

The repository owner has separately confirmed that the landed spec is a known-incomplete snapshot: it is missing a real Section 29B ("Anti-Affirmation Discipline"), full Capacity Watcher polling specifics, and a Section 36 decision log, and a corrected version will follow in a later task. Authorizing the entire eight-increment sequence against content that is already known to be missing sections would repeat exactly the failure mode Section 29A of that same document exists to prevent: treating an unverified or incomplete snapshot as durable truth.

This repository's own practice (ADR-0017 for Phase 1.5) treats a persisted, reviewable ADR — not a chat instruction — as the artifact that crosses the research-to-execution authority boundary (Section 19 of the Phase 1.6 spec; Operating law 2 of the same document: "Knowledge proposes; ADRs authorize"). No ADR currently mentions Phase 1.6.

## Decision drivers

- Phase 1.5's freeze is live-verified, not assumed, immediately before this decision.
- The repository owner has given explicit, on-the-record authorization to begin Phase 1.6 implementation, but this repository's own convention requires that authorization to exist as a durable ADR, not only as chat history.
- The landed master spec is the current planning authority but is a known-incomplete snapshot (missing Section 29B, Capacity Watcher polling detail, Section 36); pre-authorizing all eight PRs against it would authorize work against content not yet finalized.
- PR-0 (schemas, events, typed IDs, privacy/authority invariants; unit/schema tests only; no UI change) is the narrowest, most reversible increment in the sequence and does not depend on the missing sections.
- Anti-fabrication discipline governs throughout: Operating law 3 and Section 29A.4 of the spec ("no real Claude-session-cap or Codex-rate-limit event has actually been observed... this design is precautionary") — no simulated evidence may be presented as real, and simulated-vs-real must always be labeled.
- Phase 1.5 remains the execution constitution PR-0 executes through (Section 2 of the spec); this ADR does not reopen or modify frozen Phase 1.5 controls.

## Options considered

- Authorize the full PR-0 through PR-8 sequence immediately under one ADR, deferring reconciliation of the missing spec sections to whenever they arrive.
- Authorize no Phase 1.6 implementation at all until the corrected master-spec version replaces the current snapshot.
- Authorize PR-0 only — schemas, events, typed IDs, and privacy/authority invariants, unit/schema tests only, no UI change — under this ADR, and require this decision to be revisited (superseded or amended) before PR-0.5 or PR-1 begin, and in particular once the corrected spec text (real Section 29B, Capacity Watcher polling specifics, Section 36) lands.
- Treat the repository owner's chat authorization alone as sufficient and skip a persisted ADR.

## Decision

Adopt the third option: authorize starting Phase 1.6 implementation now, bounded to **PR-0 only** as Section 31 of `plans/phase-1-6-master-spec.mdoc` defines it — schemas, events, typed IDs, and privacy/authority invariants, with unit/schema tests as exit evidence and no UI change.

This ADR does **not** pre-authorize PR-0.5 through PR-8. Each subsequent increment remains gated by Section 35's execution contract (inspect current main and frozen evidence before modifying code; extend existing canonical systems rather than duplicating them; keep visual changes additive; run the inherited `ci:prepublish` and protected checks before every publication) and by Section 35.2's hard-stop conditions, including that an ADR must exist and be accepted before a material architecture decision proceeds.

Before PR-0.5 (Capacity Watcher) begins, this decision must be revisited: the corrected master-spec version (with the real Section 29B, full Capacity Watcher polling specifics, and Section 36) must have replaced the current snapshot, and PR-0.5's design must be checked against that corrected text rather than the version this ADR was written against.

PR-0 work happens under `src/modules/orchestration/` (extending the existing provider-neutral orchestration domain, not a parallel framework, per Section 35.1 point 3) or an equivalent new bounded module reviewed the same way, following this repository's established typed-contract pattern (`zod` schemas, `z.infer` exported types, deterministic unit tests) already used for `PublicationManifestSchema`, `AgentRun`, and `ProviderRun`. It inherits Phase 1.5's tool policy, publication readiness, protected GitHub checks, and attestation controls unchanged (Section 2.1, Section 27).

## Positive consequences

- Phase 1.6 implementation can begin without waiting on the corrected spec text, since PR-0's scope (contracts and invariants) does not depend on the missing sections.
- The authority boundary the spec itself insists on (Section 19: "only an accepted ADR authorizes execution") is honored literally, not treated as aspirational.
- A durable, reviewable record exists of exactly what was authorized and why, separate from conversational context that is not part of this repository's institutional memory.
- Re-authorization is explicitly required before the Capacity Watcher increment, preventing scope creep into content not yet finalized.

## Negative consequences

- An additional ADR-authoring and PR/CI/merge cycle is required before PR-0 code can start, adding latency this session.
- This ADR will likely need a follow-up decision (amendment or supersession) once the corrected spec version lands, rather than remaining a single, final record for the whole Phase 1.6 program the way ADR-0017 was for Phase 1.5's start.

## Risks

- The corrected spec version could change PR-0's own contracts in ways this ADR did not anticipate; mitigated by keeping this authorization narrowly scoped to PR-0's Section 31 definition (schemas/events/typed IDs/invariants) rather than to implementation specifics likely to shift.
- Treating "Phase 1.5 frozen" as durable rather than re-verifying it at each future PR boundary would repeat the exact staleness failure mode Section 29A of the spec documents; mitigated by citing a live, timestamped re-verification here and requiring the same discipline for every subsequent Phase 1.6 PR.
- Proceeding on a known-incomplete document risks building against sections that turn out to be materially wrong once corrected; mitigated by bounding this authorization to PR-0 only and requiring explicit revisitation before PR-0.5.

## Follow-up work

1. Implement PR-0 exactly as `plans/phase-1-6-master-spec.mdoc` Section 31 describes: schemas, events, typed IDs, and privacy/authority invariants, with unit/schema tests only and no UI change.
2. Land the corrected master-spec version (real Section 29B, Capacity Watcher polling specifics, Section 36 decision log) through the same branch/PR/CI discipline once supplied.
3. Revisit this decision (amend or supersede) before starting PR-0.5, reconciling it against the corrected spec text.
4. Re-verify Phase 1.5's frozen status live (`npm run phase15:audit`) immediately before every subsequent Phase 1.6 PR merges, per the spec's own freshness discipline — never reuse an earlier session's result.

## Supersedes or superseded by

New decision; supersedes none. It extends ADR-0017 by opening the next phase now that Phase 1.5's completion gate has verified evidence, without reopening or modifying any frozen Phase 1.5 control.

